\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% RULE RImpMood           (* imperativemood sg/pl main clause *)
< m1:I2::CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
               [ subjrel/NP{NPrec1}
                     [head/PERSPRO{PERSPROrec1}
                          [I4:head/T1] 
                     ],
                 I1:mu1,
                 auxrel/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu2],
                 mu3
               ] 
>

< m :I3::SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}
                 [ I1:mu1,
                   auxrel/VERB{VERBrec2}[mu2],
                   mu3
                 ] 
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: NOT EXIST (mu1, [subjrel/..])
       I2: CLAUSErec1.finiteness = finite  AND
           CLAUSErec1.deixis = presentdeixis AND
           CLAUSErec1.modus = imperative
       I3: SENTENCErec1.deixis = presentdeixis AND
           SENTENCErec1 = { modus      : imperative
                            finiteness : finite
                            mood       : imperativemood
                            senttype   : mainclause
                            conjkey    : 0
                            adverbial  : false
                            temporal   : false
                            conjclass  : omegaTimeAdvClass
                            conjdeixis : omegadeixis
                            conjaspect : omegaAspect
                            conjretro  : false }
       I4: T1.CAT = BPERSPRO
      >

   PARAMETERS
      <
       numberpar : numbertype
      >

<
   SUBRULE (* 1. singular *)
      < T1: BPERSPRO(KEY_yousgBPERSPROkey){BPERSPROrec1} >
      < >

      COMP
         <
          C1: VERBrec1 = { modus   : omegamodus 
                           persons NI 2
                           numbers NI singular } AND
              PERSPROrec1 = COPYT_bpersproTOperspro(BPERSPROrec1) AND
              PERSPROrec1.persprocase = Nominative AND 
              NPrec1 = AUX_NPONyousgPRSPR EXCEPT FOR [cases] AND
              NPrec1.cases = [ nominative] AND
              numberpar = singular 
          A1: VERBrec2 := VERBrec1;
              VERBrec2 := { modus   : infinitive
                            persons : [] 
                            numbers : [] };
              SENTENCErec1 := COPYT_clauseTOsentence(CLAUSErec1);
              SENTENCErec1 := { mood     : imperativemood
                                senttype : mainclause
                                modus    : imperative }
              (* other attributes specific to sentence 
                 retain default values *)
         >

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: VERBrec2 = { modus   : infinitive
                           persons : [] 
                           numbers : [] }
          A1: VERBrec1 := VERBrec2;
              VERBrec1 := { numbers : [singular]
                            modus   : omegamodus
                            persons : [2] } ;
              CLAUSErec1 := COPYT_sentenceTOclause(SENTENCErec1);
              (* including modus, finiteness and deixis *)
              PERSPROrec1 := COPYT_bpersproTOperspro(BPERSPROrec1);
              PERSPROrec1.persprocase := Nominative;
              NPrec1 := AUX_NPONyousgPRSPR;
              NPrec1.cases := [nominative];
              numberpar := singular
         >

   SUBRULE (* 2. plural *)
      < T1: BPERSPRO(KEY_youplBPERSPROkey){BPERSPROrec1} >
      < >

      COMP
         <
          C1: VERBrec1 = { modus   : omegamodus 
                           persons NI 2
                           numbers NI plural } AND
              PERSPROrec1 = COPYT_bpersproTOperspro(BPERSPROrec1) AND
              PERSPROrec1.persprocase = Nominative AND 
              NPrec1 = AUX_NPONyouplPRSPR EXCEPT FOR [cases] AND
              NPrec1.cases = [nominative] AND
              numberpar = plural
          A1: VERBrec2 := VERBrec1;
              VERBrec2 := { modus   : infinitive
                            persons : [] 
                            numbers : [] };
              SENTENCErec1 := COPYT_clauseTOsentence(CLAUSErec1);
              SENTENCErec1 := { mood     : imperativemood
                                senttype : mainclause
                                modus    : imperative }
              (* other attributes specific to sentence 
                 retain default values *)
         >

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: VERBrec2 = { modus   : infinitive
                           persons : [] 
                           numbers : [] }
          A1: VERBrec1 := VERBrec2;
              VERBrec1 := { numbers : [plural]
                            modus   : omegamodus
                            persons : [2] } ;
              CLAUSErec1 := COPYT_sentenceTOclause(SENTENCErec1);
              (* including modus, finiteness and deixis *)
              PERSPROrec1 := COPYT_bpersproTOperspro(BPERSPROrec1);
              PERSPROrec1.persprocase := Nominative;
              NPrec1 := AUX_NPONyouplPRSPR;
              NPrec1.cases := [nominative];
              numberpar := plural
         >
>
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RImpMood
\item[* relevant examples:] \mbox{}\\
$_{CL}$[subjrel/you do buy a book for John] 
$\rightarrow$ $_{S}$[ do buy a book for John]  (uiteindelijk: buy a book for 
John!)\\
$_{CL}$[subjrel/you do behave yourselves] 
$\rightarrow$ $_{S}$[ do behave yourselves]  (uiteindelijk: Behave yourselves!)
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] CLAUSEtoSENTENCE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC\_
MoodDetermination
\item[* date of creation:] 3-MAY-1988
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch Rmood7
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To make an imperative singular or plural main clause
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) Note that in 
analysis, an ambiguity is created between singular and plural imperatives. This 
ambiguity will not be solved in the subject-verb agreement rule, since the verb 
form does not show any difference. Only in case an inherent reflexive exists, 
will the Reflexive spelling rule (in VerbppropFormation only!) solve the 
ambiguity; otherwise, two paths continue all the way to IL.
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:] one subrule for 
each value of the parameter (singular, plural)
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of 
parameters) same syntax for singular and plural
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] none
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\
There are two {\em you\/}s in the dictionary: a singular one and a plural one.
\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\
No rule has been written yet for English constructions like {\em Do wash 
yourselves, Do be quiet, Do come in\/}. They might be made in a special POSImp 
mood rule, which intriduces POS to prevent Do-deletion. The Dutch translation 
would be something with {\em alsjeblieft: Was jezelf alsjeblieft\/}, or perhaps 
{\em toch: Wees toch stil\/}. (What about Spanish?)\\
Perhaps there is interrelation with another kind of imperative in Dutch, the 
{\em U-imp: Gaat U verder, Komt U binnen\/}, which I think would best translate 
into an English {\em Please-Imp: Please enter\/}, or, alternatively, into the 
POS-Imp just mentioned.
\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\

\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

